      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The bilateral administration of the serotonin receptor antagonist methysergide (2.5 microg, 5 microg, and 10 microg) into either the central nucleus of the amygdala (ACe) or nucleus parafascicularis thalami (nPf) produced dose-dependent inhibition of the antinociceptive action of ventrolateral periaqueductal gray (vPAG)-administered morphine. Unilateral administration of these doses of methysergide into either the ACe or nPf had no effect on morphine-induced antinociception. However, the combined unilateral administration of these doses of methysergide into the ACe and nPf produced dose-dependent inhibition of morphine antinociception that was identical to that observed after its bilateral administration into either site. This latter finding is interpreted as evidence that a functional interaction between the ACe and nPf supports the antinociceptive action of morphine administered into the vPAG."},
